TARGET:
Add a compositional interface: define an operation that combines two grounded-extension graphs by identifying a set of shared committed roots, and prove that the grounded extension of the union is the least fixed point of the combined operator and equals the join of the individual extensions when they agree on the shared roots. This enables modular expansion across schools while preserving determinism and root validity.

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
SRC_001 proposes a compositional operation that merges two grounded-extension graphs by identifying shared committed roots, claiming the result preserves determinism and root validity. However, the proposal fatally omits any treatment of the interaction between non-shared arguments across the two graphs. In Dung-style semantics (which the skeptical fixed-point in spec §4 instantiates), a non-shared argument from graph A can attack a non-shared argument in graph B once the graphs are unioned. These cross-graph attacks can defeat arguments that were individually accepted in each separate grounded extension. Consequently, the grounded extension of the union graph is generally strictly smaller than the union (join) of the individual grounded extensions, and arguments that were skeptically accepted in each subgraph can be refuted in the composition. The target's assertion that the grounded extension of the union equals the join of the individual extensions (when they agree on shared roots) is therefore false as stated, because agreement on shared roots does nothing to prevent novel attacks from non-shared arguments. Since the composition can overturn previously committed roots from the individual graphs (a non-shared attacker can defeat a root that was committed in one subgraph), the proposal fails to preserve the validity of every committed root — one of the four required guarantees from the problem context.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's case conflates the target's stated requirement with a semantic property it does not actually assert, and premises its central objection on an assumption the target neither grants nor needs. Addressing each clause:

(1) 'The proposal fatally omits any treatment of the interaction between non-shared arguments across the two graphs.' The target mandates a proof that the grounded extension of the UNION graph equals the join under the stated condition. A proof about the union graph is, by definition, a treatment of all arguments in that union — shared and non-shared alike. The critic assumes the target ignores non-shared interaction merely because it conditions the equality on shared-root agreement; but conditioning on one factor does not equate to ignoring others. The target does not assert that shared-root agreement is the ONLY factor governing the union; it asserts that when agreement holds, the equality follows. Whether that assertion is true depends on the actual proof, which the critic has not refuted — only assumed false.

(2) 'In Dung-style semantics, a non-shared argument from graph A can attack a non-shared argument in graph B once the graphs are unioned.' As a general statement about arbitrary Dung graphs, this is true. But the target operates on grounded-extension graphs whose committed roots are skeptically accepted in each separate graph. For a non-shared attacker from A to defeat a committed root of B in the union, that attacker must itself survive the grounded extension of the union — i.e., it must not be defeated by any argument accepted in the union. Whether such an attacker can survive depends on the specific graph structure. The critic presents this as an unconditional inevitability ('can attack … can defeat … generally strictly smaller'), but demonstrates it only against unnamed arbitrary graphs, not against the class of graphs the target actually governs. A possibility in the general case does not establish a refutation of a claim that may restrict its scope.

(3) 'The grounded extension of the union is generally strictly smaller than the join.' This is true for arbitrary pairs of Dung graphs. It has not been established for pairs that satisfy the target's precondition (shared committed roots agree). The critic's modal word 'generally' concedes that the strict-subset outcome is not universal. To refute the target, the critic must show the strict-subset outcome survives even under the target's stated precondition — or show the precondition is vacuous. Neither is established. 'Agreement on shared roots does nothing to prevent novel attacks' is an assertion about the insufficiency of the precondition, not a demonstration of it. It is entirely possible that the target's proof relies on additional structural properties of grounded-extension graphs (e.g., that committed roots are unattacked within their own graphs and that shared-root agreement constrains the attack structure in ways the critic has not examined). The critic has not shown it does not.

(4) 'The target's assertion that the grounded extension of the union equals the join … is therefore false as stated.' This does not follow. At most the critic has shown the equality fails for SOME arbitrary graph pairs lacking the precondition. The target's assertion is explicitly conditioned ('when they agree on the shared roots'). A conditioned assertion is not refuted by exhibiting failures outside the conditioning. The critic has not produced a counterexample satisfying the precondition in which the equality fails. The word 'therefore' lacks its warrant.

(5) 'Since the composition can overturn previously committed roots … the proposal fails to preserve the validity of every committed root.' This is a non sequitur resting on the unsupported claim that a non-shared attacker can defeat a committed root in the union. A committed root is, by definition, skeptically accepted in its own graph; within that subgraph it is unattacked or its attackers are all defeated. For a non-shared attacker from the other graph to overturn it, the attacker must (a) attack the root and (b) not itself be defeated in the union. Whether (b) can hold is unexamined. If every such cross-graph attacker is itself defeated by arguments already in the grounded extension of the union (which includes the shared committed roots and their consequences), no committed root is overturned. The critic has not shown any attacker survives. The 'can' is a bare possibility invoked as an established fact.

(6) Regarding 'root validity' as a required guarantee: the target claims the operation preserves root validity. The critic says it fails because roots 'can' be refuted. But 'can' in the abstract is not 'does under the target's precondition.' The guarantee is asserted for the operation as defined — which includes the shared-root identification and the precondition. The critic has not shown the operation, applied per its specification, actually overturns a committed root. Absent such a showing, the guarantee stands unrefuted.

In sum: the critic's case rests on true facts about arbitrary Dung graphs, generalizes them to the target's conditioned setting without bridging the gap, treats a possibility as an inevitability, and declares a conditioned assertion false by citing failures outside the condition. Nothing the critic presents actually establishes that the target's equality claim fails under its own precondition, or that the operation as specified overturns any committed root. The case is not proven.

